<h1>mcp-librarian Wiki</h1>
<p class="subtitle">What version 3.1.0 actually does, including the parts callers still have to enforce.</p>

<div class="warning-box"><strong>Experimental:</strong> the repository's package.json is 3.1.0, but the current MCP initialize response and <code>server_status</code> still report <code>3.0.0</code>. That is a known implementation bug, not a second release line.</div>

<h2 id="what-it-is">What it is</h2>
<p>mcp-librarian is a zero-external-runtime-dependency Node.js MCP server. It stores flat Markdown skill files under <code>~/.mcp-librarian/skills/</code>, builds an in-memory BM25 index, and exposes {{ versions.librarian.tools }} tools over stdio.</p>
<p>Search and loading are local. Four dedicated tools use the network: <code>browse_packs</code>, <code>install_pack</code>, <code>research_topic</code>, and <code>fetch_page</code>. In addition, <code>find_and_load</code> can call the research path when <code>autoResearch</code> is explicitly enabled; it is off by default.</p>

<h2 id="retrieval">Retrieval semantics</h2>
<ul>
  <li><code>find_skill</code> returns ranked section-level matches with snippets and scores.</li>
  <li><code>load_section</code> returns one exact section by slug.</li>
  <li><code>load_skill</code> returns a full skill file plus its section slugs.</li>
  <li><code>find_and_load</code> returns the <strong>full top skill</strong> when the raw BM25 score clears its threshold. It does not return only the matched section.</li>
</ul>

<h2 id="integrity">Integrity is a status, not an enforced load gate</h2>
<p>If <code>keys/private.pem</code> exists, supported writes re-sign the flat skill library. At startup or rebuild, the store compares files with <code>manifest.json</code> and records <code>VERIFIED</code>, <code>TAMPERED</code>, or <code>UNSIGNED</code>. <code>list_skills</code> and <code>skill_status</code> expose that snapshot.</p>
<p>Current <code>load_skill</code>, <code>load_section</code>, and <code>find_and_load</code> responses do not refuse tampered content or reliably include its integrity status. There is no file watcher. A caller that needs this boundary must check status and decide what to reject.</p>

<h2 id="packs">Current pack registry</h2>
<p>The checked-in community registry currently has {{ versions.librarian.packs }} packs and {{ versions.librarian.packSkills }} skill files: one file per pack. A pack install can accept safe files while rejecting other files, and a same-name incoming skill can update an existing local skill. Read the <a href="/librarian/wiki/community-packs/">Community Packs</a> page before treating installs as atomic or non-destructive.</p>

